  "answer_text": "When receiving food deliveries\u2014especially **TCS (Time/Temperature Control for Safety)** foods\u2014the acceptable temperatures for safe receipt depend on whether the food is cold, hot, or frozen:\n\n### 1. Cold TCS Foods (General Rule: 41\u00b0F / 5\u00b0C or lower)\nMost refrigerated, perishable foods (such as fresh meats, poultry, fish, dairy, and pre-cut produce) must be received at **41\u00b0F (5\u00b0C) or lower**. \n\nHowever, there are a few **regulatory exceptions** that can be received at slightly warmer temperatures, provided they are quickly cooled:\n*   **Milk:** Can be received at **45\u00b0F (7\u00b0C) or lower**, but must be cooled to 41\u00b0F (5\u00b0C) or lower within 4 hours of receipt.\n*   **Shell Eggs:** Must be received at an air temperature of **45\u00b0F (7\u00b0C) or lower**.\n*   **Shucked Shellfish:** Can be received at **45\u00b0F (7\u00b0C) or lower**, but must be cooled to 41\u00b0F (5\u00b0C) or lower within 4 hours.\n*   **Live Shellfish (Oysters, Clams, Mussels, Scallops):** Must be received at an air temperature of **45\u00b0F (7\u00b0C)** and an internal temperature of no greater than **50\u00b0F (10\u00b0C)**. They must be cooled to 41\u00b0F (5\u00b0C) or lower within 4 hours.\n\n### 2. Hot TCS Foods (135\u00b0F / 57\u00b0C or higher)\nIf you are receiving hot, prepared foods (such as pre-cooked meals or catered items), they must be received at **135\u00b0F (57\u00b0C) or higher**. \n\n### 3. Frozen Foods (Frozen Solid)\nFrozen foods do not have a specific numerical temperature requirement, but they must be **frozen solid** when they arrive. \n\n**You should reject frozen food if you notice:**\n*   Ice crystals or frozen liquids on the food or the packaging (this is a sign of thawing and refreezing, which is a major food safety hazard).\n*   Fluids or water stains on the bottom of the cases or packaging.\n\n---\n\n### How to Properly Check the Temperature of Received Food\n*   **Meat, Poultry, and Fish:** Insert the thermometer stem or probe directly into the thickest part of the food.\n*   **Reduced-Oxygen Packaged (ROP) Food (e.g., vacuum-packed or sous vide):** Insert the thermometer stem or probe between two packages. *Do not puncture the packaging*.\n*   **Other Packaged Foods (e.g., milk cartons, tubs of sour cream):** Open the package and insert the thermometer stem/probe directly into the food without letting it touch the sides or bottom of the packaging.",
